import{_ as a,P as t,d as i,e as o,y as s,q as r,f as d}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const y=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.23 Family, Gender, the Body, Dependence, and Care","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.23 Family, Gender, the Body, Dependence, and Care","xkChars":0,"xkWords":473,"xkLessons":{"D.23":{"title":"D.23 Family, Gender, the Body, Dependence, and Care","lead":"Dependence and autonomy can coexist. Trace care support to who actually decides, who performs the work, and whether the burden is shifted to another household.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 4973–4988, original site location /theory/appendix-d/d-23#section-d-23. See the theory-v2 source for the current complete text; the historical research manuscript and excerpts remain available to download and consult in their original edition.","Brief quotation, translated from Chinese: ‘Dependence and autonomy can coexist.’ Marriage, kinship, co-residence, intimacy, property rights, care, and legal duties are distinct relationships. Relatives need not live together, and co-residents need not be intimate; total household resources do not mean every member can freely use them. Remittances across locations can provide material support, but their amount cannot confirm that daily care has been taken over. [T-main]","Patriarchy, social reproduction, feminist traditions, and work–family research offer different entry points. A single gender essence cannot explain all labor and experience. Examine how rules allocate time, bodily labor, resources, and voice while retaining individual experience and relational meaning; structural analysis must not erase subjects' feelings. [T-main]","Care burdens include not only paid hours but also primary care, simultaneous tasks, planning and coordination, being on call, lost sleep, and future opportunities. Their importance does not mean all durations can be added directly: an hour of supervision may overlap with cooking. On-call constraints must be recorded separately, not disguised as additional clock hours. [T-main]","The dependence of occupational returns on continuous attendance and time flexibility must be examined alongside job design, employer constraints, public support, and discrimination. An observed income gap establishes a descriptive difference, but alone cannot establish natural ability, a unique cause, or fairness. References here to Goldin and others identify research directions; they do not claim a complete review of all their research. [T-main]","Intimate conflict, sustained control, and immediate danger cannot share a single response template. Communication or joint therapy cannot be recommended uniformly, and safe exit need not wait until every ideal resource is available. Continuing limited responsibilities should involve arranging other conditions for taking them over; continued exposure to danger cannot be made a cost the victim must bear. [T-main]","A child's expression, the weight of their views, consent to a particular matter, and final decision-making authority must be separated. First provide support for understanding and expression; temporary inability to express oneself independently cannot justify withholding needed help. Providing support does not automatically confer all powers to decide in the person's place. This is conceptual analysis; actual professional or legal matters still require the relevant procedures. [T-main]","The source's teaching example of an assisted arrangement shows that a person needing daily support can still participate in decisions. Autonomy does not mean complete independence from others, and a helper's ability does not create unlimited obligations. Experiences of the body, sexuality, illness, aging, and disability coexist with institutional barriers; medical treatment, improvements in daily life, and administrative eligibility cannot prove one another. [T-main]","Specific historical arrangement: a 2005 Cash and Counseling report describes programs in Arkansas, New Jersey, and Florida in which counseling, fiscal services, and other support assist participants with spending plans and employer roles. The design distinguishes participation in decisions from support for execution; fully independent execution is not made a conceptual prerequisite for participation in decisions. [P-CASH]","The two specified pages of the original support only the account of arrangements and role division. A spending plan alone cannot establish that every participant can decide freely, and the existence of fiscal support does not imply reduced total burdens for families, grandparents, employees, or counselors. The additional question here is whether roles and support can connect, rather than treating the program's name as certification of every outcome. [P-CASH]","Time saved by one household may come from increased work by another household, grandparents, or paid carers. Preserve both the benefit and the destination of the burden; do not count every transfer as a net reduction. Meaningful work can also involve difficulty refusing. That difficulty needs actual evidence and cannot be inferred solely from family status. [T-main]","Kinship, households, residences, and care networks spanning households have different boundaries. Means from two individually valid populations cannot be combined into records for paired spouses: paired units and a common time basis are required. Connections to §§9.7–9.8 retain conditions and experience; §§10.5–10.8 examine overlapping power and support across circles; and §14.3 and §§15.3.1–15.3.2 trace responsibility and the actual conditions of subjects. [T-main]","The eight entry points are: 23.01, boundaries of marriage and kinship institutions; 23.02, gendered divisions of labor and labor reproduction; 23.03, different feminist questions and evidence; 23.04, distinctions among conflict, control, violence, and safe exit; 23.05, support for expression during development and intergenerational power; 23.06, bodily norms and self-determination; 23.07, support and barriers in illness, aging, and disability; and 23.08, work–family time arrangements and mechanisms of returns. This explains the navigation, not a comprehensive review of all eight fields. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Map kinship, households, support networks, and decision-making powers separately.","Record actual care, simultaneous tasks, on-call demands, and opportunity constraints without double counting.","Check who can decide, who can refuse, and the boundaries of support roles.","Distinguish safety risks from ordinary conflict; do not make continued exposure a condition of continuing responsibility.","Trace work across households, assessing local improvement and total burden separately."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Dependence","leftText":"Needing support from others.","rightTitle":"Autonomy","rightText":"Conditions for participating in decisions and directing oneself; autonomy and dependence can coexist."},{"leftTitle":"Household","leftText":"A specified statistical boundary for shared living.","rightTitle":"Care network","rightText":"May span households and locations; its boundary is not automatically the same."},{"leftTitle":"Reduced work hours","leftText":"Less time spent by a particular actor on a particular task.","rightTitle":"Net burden reduction","rightText":"Still requires checking additional work by others and double counting of simultaneous tasks."},{"leftTitle":"Expressing a view","leftText":"Making one's meaning understandable.","rightTitle":"Final decision-making authority","rightText":"Requires a separate check for the matter and procedure concerned."}],"boundaries":["Teaching counterexample from the source: a person still needs daily support but can participate in decisions through assisted arrangements.","Source boundary: time saved by one household may depend on additional work by another household or a paid carer.","Teaching counterexample: means from two unpaired populations are combined as though they were records of a married couple."],"questions":["Does dependence mean a lack of autonomy?","If household A saves four hours, is the total reduction four hours?","Does a helper's ability require unlimited assistance?","Can support be refused because someone cannot express themselves independently?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-23/figures/d23-relations.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Decision and support arrangements in the 2005 three-state Cash and Counseling programs","href":"https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/migrated_legacy_files/41521/adultpcw.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Decision and support arrangements in the 2005 three-state Cash and Counseling programs","text":"The report's two specified pages describe spending plans, employer roles, counseling, and fiscal support in the Arkansas, New Jersey, and Florida programs.","sourceLabel":"Effect of Consumer Direction on Adults’ Personal Care and Well-Being in Arkansas, New Jersey and Florida","sourceUrl":"https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/migrated_legacy_files/41521/adultpcw.pdf","sourceLocator":"Report dated 2005-05-16, PDF pages 19–20 (printed pages 7–8), program service arrangements","boundary":"Institutional arrangements do not prove actual autonomy for everyone or a fall in the total care burden. The lesson's source record reports a 403 response on an online refetch and a reading of the two specified pages in an existing original PDF.","href":"https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/migrated_legacy_files/41521/adultpcw.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.23","start_line":5064,"end_line":5079,"startLine":5064,"endLine":5079}],"outputId":"section-d-23","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: transfer and net reduction","text":"In a fictional example, household A initially provides 10 hours of care. After a change, A provides 6 hours and household B adds 3. With a consistent measure and no overlapping work, the total falls from 10 to 9: a net reduction of 1 hour. A's four-hour saving cannot all count as a net social reduction. If B instead adds 5 hours, the total is 11: A still saves 4 while the total increases by 1.","assumptions":"Two artificial households, no other work, a common time window, and no double counting of simultaneous tasks.","boundary":"The meaning of the work and harms are not counted; total hours alone cannot determine legitimacy."},{"title":"Teaching construction: simultaneous tasks cannot be counted twice as clock time","text":"Within an artificial eight-hour care window, two hours also involve cooking. Eight hours of care plus two of cooking make 10 task-hours, but their union in clock time remains eight hours. To describe multitasking intensity, report two simultaneous hours; do not write the union as 10 hours as well.","assumptions":"Cooking is entirely contained within the eight-hour window; the intervals are purely artificial.","boundary":"This does not mean simultaneous work is not tiring, or cancel effects on on-call demands and sleep."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample from the source: a person still needs daily support but can participate in decisions through assisted arrangements.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: time saved by one household may depend on additional work by another household or a paid carer.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: means from two unpaired populations are combined as though they were records of a married couple.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does dependence mean a lack of autonomy?","answer":"No. Check participation in decisions and the conditions of support.","basis":"The source's central point."},{"question":"If household A saves four hours, is the total reduction four hours?","answer":"Not necessarily. Additional work in other households and the treatment of overlap must also be checked.","basis":"Burdens across boundaries."},{"question":"Does a helper's ability require unlimited assistance?","answer":"Ability does not directly imply unlimited obligation; responsibility has its own boundaries.","basis":"Responsibility."},{"question":"Can support be refused because someone cannot express themselves independently?","answer":"That cannot instead become grounds to withdraw needed support for understanding and expression.","basis":"Support comes first."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-23/figures/d23-relations.png","alt":"Check marriage, kinship, households, care duties, and control of resources separately.","caption":"Based on D.23."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-23/figures/d23-support.png","alt":"Separate participation in decisions from counseling, fiscal services, and daily support; support does not confer unlimited powers.","caption":"Specified arrangements in the 2005 program and theoretical boundaries."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-23/figures/d23-time.png","alt":"A falls from 10 to 6, B adds 3, and the total is 9; simultaneous tasks differ from clock time.","caption":"Teaching construction."}],"diagramBrief":"Three figures show relational boundaries, dependence and autonomy, and a cross-household time calculation.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["The program's complete empirical results and changes in each household's burden were not evaluated.","No professional judgment is made about individual medical matters, decisions involving children, or family law."],"taskId":"S-D-23","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§D.23, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 4885–4900","claim_support":["Multiple family boundaries, care measurement, autonomy and dependence, safe exit, support powers, and eight entry points."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-CASH","title":"Effect of Consumer Direction on Adults’ Personal Care and Well-Being in Arkansas, New Jersey and Florida","url":"https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/migrated_legacy_files/41521/adultpcw.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2005-05-16","locator":"Report dated 2005-05-16, PDF pages 19–20 (printed pages 7–8), program service arrangements","claim_support":["Spending plans, employer roles, counseling, and fiscal support arrangements in the Arkansas, New Jersey, and Florida programs."],"limits":["The lesson author read the two specified pages of an existing original PDF in full. A refetch from the official site returned 403; this was not taken to mean the full text was unverifiable. Only institutional arrangements are cited here, without claiming actual autonomy for every participant or reduced care burdens."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"D.23 Family, Gender, the Body, Dependence, and Care; Chinese source lines 4973–4988; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-23/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-23/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-23/source/S-D-23.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-23/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"85b7363070299bf1a7e0cbbe3319782a76d3224a72ef4aa6b7b2cee56b23e150","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-23.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-23.md"}`),l={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-23.md"};function c(u,e,p,h,m,f){const n=t("SectionLesson");return i(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s("",1)),r(n,{section:"D.23"}),e[1]||(e[1]=d("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const b=a(l,[["render",c]]);export{y as __pageData,b as default};
